## The Setting Filter

**The Setting Filter is how ring setting choice amplifies or dampens the moissanite fire effect.** You control this at the order stage, after stone selection. Use it correctly and you can shift the fire character significantly.

**Metal color** changes fire perception. White gold and platinum frame rainbow arcs with a neutral surround — the colors appear clearly against cool metal. Yellow gold and rose gold add warm amber tones that blend with the warmer fire spectrum. The result: fire that feels amber-warm rather than electric-rainbow.

**Accent diamonds** compete with moissanite fire and reduce its dominance. A three-stone setting with diamond flankers splits your attention between the moissanite centre and the diamond sparkle. Diamond sparkle is white — it blends with moissanite brilliance, not the color fire. The rainbow becomes one element rather than the whole show.

**Setting silhouette** changes fire directionality. A solitaire puts no competition around the moissanite — maximum fire. A halo redirects some fire into the surrounding stones. A bezel partially contains side fire and focuses what you see through the open table.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Setting Style</th><th>Fire Effect</th><th>Best Metal for Moderation</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Solitaire</strong></td><td>Maximum — nothing competes</td><td>Rose or yellow gold</td><td>Full fire at all times. Choose only if you love the effect.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Three stone with diamonds</strong></td><td>Moderate — flankers split attention</td><td>White or rose gold</td><td>Best balance. Most popular moissanite setting choice.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pavé with diamond shoulders</strong></td><td>Reduced — shoulder sparkle competes</td><td>Any</td><td>Diamond shoulder fire blends with moissanite. Busy but balanced.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Halo with diamonds</strong></td><td>Layered — halo and centre fire together</td><td>White gold</td><td>Maximum visual size. Complex layered fire pattern.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bezel</strong></td><td>Focused — metal contains side fire</td><td>Any</td><td>Quietest fire. Best for buyers who want restraint. See [bezel guide](/marquise-bezel-engagement-ring).</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** Three stone with diamond flankers in rose gold is the best Setting Filter combination. The diamond flankers split fire attention, the rose gold warms the spectrum, and the marquise centre still delivers its elongated face-up presence. Entry at [**$1,620 (Three Stone Rose Gold)**](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/marquise-cut-lab-grown-diamond-three-stone-engagement-ring-in-14k-rose-gold-by-james-allen-item-315101?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) with 133 reviews. If you want maximum fire, choose the solitaire. If you want the fire quieter, choose bezel. See our [marquise halo engagement ring guide](/marquise-halo-engagement-ring) for halo fire-layering options.

## Marquise Moissanite vs Lab Diamond vs Natural Diamond

Most buyers comparing these three options want the same thing: the best-looking marquise ring for their budget. Here is what that actually looks like in numbers.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Size</th><th>Moissanite Stone</th><th>Lab Diamond Stone</th><th>Natural Diamond</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>1.00ct</strong></td><td>~$449 (Forever One DEF)</td><td>~$800–$1,200 (IGI E-VS1)</td><td>~$4,000–$6,000 (G-VS2)</td><td>Moissanite saves 50–55% vs lab, 92% vs natural.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>1.50ct</strong></td><td>~$729</td><td>~$1,400–$2,000</td><td>~$8,000–$12,000</td><td>Savings grow with size. Same relative advantage.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2.00ct</strong></td><td>~$949</td><td>[~$2,212 IGI E-VS1](/2-carat-lab-marquise-diamond)</td><td>~$15,000</td><td>Moissanite saves 57% vs lab, 94% vs natural at 2ct.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>3.00ct</strong></td><td>~$1,449</td><td>[~$3,819 IGI E-VS1](/3-carat-lab-marquise-diamond)</td><td>~$25,000+</td><td>At 3ct, moissanite is 62% below lab and 94% below natural.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** Moissanite saves 50–62% vs lab diamond at every carat weight. Lab diamond saves 73–94% vs natural. The fire character separates moissanite from lab — not price alone. If diamond fire character matters to you, choose lab at $800–$3,819. If savings matter above fire character, moissanite at $449–$1,449 gives you more stone for less.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Size</th><th>Moissanite Ring Total</th><th>Lab Diamond Ring Total</th><th>Natural Ring Total</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>1ct + Three Stone $1,620</strong></td><td>~$2,069</td><td>~$2,420–$2,820</td><td>~$5,620–$7,620</td><td>Moissanite saves $350–$750 vs lab at 1ct. Natural is 3x more.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>2ct + Side Stone $2,350</strong></td><td>~$3,299</td><td>[~$4,562](/2-carat-lab-marquise-diamond)</td><td>~$17,350</td><td>Gap widens at 2ct. Moissanite saves $1,263 vs lab.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3ct + Side Stone $2,350</strong></td><td>~$3,799</td><td>[~$6,169](/3-carat-lab-marquise-diamond)</td><td>~$27,350+</td><td>At 3ct, moissanite saves $2,370 vs lab. Natural is 7x moissanite price.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** At 1ct, the total ring difference between moissanite and lab is $350–$750. At 3ct, it is $2,370. The fire difference exists at all sizes. At 1ct, the savings may not justify the fire difference if a lab diamond ring fits your budget. At 2ct+, the savings become large enough to reconsider even for buyers who prefer lab diamond character.

## Moissanite Grading and the Forever One DEF Standard

Moissanite does not use [GIA](https://www.gia.edu/) color and clarity grading. GIA grades diamonds. [Charles & Colvard](https://www.charlesandcolvard.com/) developed the Forever One standard for moissanite — the industry benchmark. Each Forever One stone ships with a C&C certificate confirming the grade.

Forever One DEF corresponds to the D-E-F colorless range on the GIA color scale. Premium grade corresponds to G-H-I. Classic corresponds to J-K.

For a marquise moissanite ring, Forever One DEF is the correct choice. The marquise's long face-up surface amplifies color at the pointed ends. A G-H range stone will show yellow-green tint in white gold or platinum at 2ct+. DEF stays clean in any metal.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Grade</th><th>GIA Color Equiv.</th><th>1ct Price</th><th>Metal Compatibility</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Forever One DEF</strong></td><td>D-E-F (colorless)</td><td>~$449</td><td>All metals</td><td>Our pick. Clean neutral in any metal. No visible tint.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Premium (GHI)</strong></td><td>G-H-I (near-colorless)</td><td>~$349–$400</td><td>Yellow gold, rose gold</td><td>Slight warmth. Acceptable in warm metals. Avoid platinum.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Classic</strong></td><td>J-K range</td><td>~$250–$320</td><td>Yellow gold only</td><td>Visible warm tint. Not recommended for white metals.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** Forever One DEF for every marquise moissanite ring. The price gap between DEF and Premium is $50–$100 per carat. At $449 vs ~$380 for 1ct, the $69 premium buys a stone that reads clean in any metal. At 2ct+ in a marquise, the color difference at the ends is visible enough to matter. See our [marquise diamond colour guide](/marquise-diamond-color-guide) for the same color principles applied to diamonds.

